      <span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 6 · Lesson 3</span>
      <h1>Evaluation Metrics for <span class="accent">Regression</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Master the essential metrics for evaluating regression models, from simple distance-based measures to robust error metrics and R-squared variants.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Learning Objectives -->
    <div class="objectives">
      <h4>Learning Objectives</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Understand MAE, MSE, and RMSE and when to use each</li>
        <li>Interpret R-squared (coefficient of determination)</li>
        <li>Use MAPE for percentage error interpretability</li>
        <li>Handle outliers with robust metrics</li>
        <li>Select appropriate metrics based on problem context</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Regression vs Classification -->
    <section class="section" id="regression-intro">
      <span class="section-label">Foundation Concepts</span>
      <h2>Why Regression Metrics Differ from Classification</h2>
      <p>Regression predicts continuous values (house prices, temperature, stock prices) rather than discrete classes. This fundamental difference requires different evaluation metrics. While classification metrics (accuracy, precision, recall) deal with correct/incorrect predictions, regression metrics measure how close predictions are to actual values.</p>

      <p>A prediction that's off by $5,000 might be acceptable when predicting a $500,000 house but terrible when predicting a $10,000 car. Context matters enormously in regression evaluation, more so than in classification where most metrics are scale-independent.</p>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> Regression metrics quantify prediction error. Lower is better for all distance-based metrics (MAE, MSE, RMSE). Higher is better for correlation-based metrics (R-squared).
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- MAE and MSE -->
    <section class="section" id="mae-mse">
      <span class="section-label">Core Metrics</span>
      <h2>Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Mean Squared Error (MSE)</h2>
      <p><strong>Mean Absolute Error (MAE)</strong> is the average absolute difference between predictions and actual values. It's intuitive and robust to outliers because it uses absolute differences rather than squared differences.</p>

      <p>Formula: MAE = (1/n) × Σ|y_actual - y_predicted|</p>

      <p><strong>Mean Squared Error (MSE)</strong> squares each error before averaging. This amplifies the impact of large errors, making MSE sensitive to outliers. MSE is more commonly used in optimization because it's mathematically convenient and differentiable, but it's less interpretable because it's in squared units.</p>

      <p>Formula: MSE = (1/n) × Σ(y_actual - y_predicted)²</p>

      <div class="diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 900 280" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <!-- Title -->
          <text x="300" y="25" font-size="18" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2937">Error Metrics Comparison</text>

          <!-- MAE box -->
          <rect x="50" y="50" width="230" height="200" fill="#f3f4f6" stroke="#e5e7eb" stroke-width="1" rx="4"/>
          <text x="70" y="70" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#1f2937">Mean Absolute Error (MAE)</text>

          <text x="70" y="95" font-size="11" font-weight="500" fill="#4b5563">Advantages:</text>
          <text x="85" y="115" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Units are interpretable</text>
          <text x="85" y="130" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Robust to outliers</text>
          <text x="85" y="145" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Easy to explain</text>

          <text x="70" y="170" font-size="11" font-weight="500" fill="#4b5563">Disadvantages:</text>
          <text x="85" y="190" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Not differentiable at zero</text>
          <text x="85" y="205" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Less suitable for optimization</text>

          <!-- MSE box -->
          <rect x="320" y="50" width="230" height="200" fill="#f3f4f6" stroke="#e5e7eb" stroke-width="1" rx="4"/>
          <text x="340" y="70" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#1f2937">Mean Squared Error (MSE)</text>

          <text x="340" y="95" font-size="11" font-weight="500" fill="#4b5563">Advantages:</text>
          <text x="355" y="115" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Differentiable everywhere</text>
          <text x="355" y="130" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Good for optimization</text>
          <text x="355" y="145" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Mathematically convenient</text>

          <text x="340" y="170" font-size="11" font-weight="500" fill="#4b5563">Disadvantages:</text>
          <text x="355" y="190" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Sensitive to outliers</text>
          <text x="355" y="205" font-size="10" fill="#4b5563">• Hard to interpret (squared units)</text>
        </svg>
      </div>

      <h3>Computing MAE and MSE</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">mean_absolute_error</span>, <span class="fn">mean_squared_error</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Example: House price prediction</span>
<span class="va">y_actual</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">300000</span>, <span class="nm">250000</span>, <span class="nm">400000</span>, <span class="nm">350000</span>])
<span class="va">y_pred</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">310000</span>, <span class="nm">240000</span>, <span class="nm">420000</span>, <span class="nm">340000</span>])

<span class="cm"># MAE: average error in dollars</span>
<span class="va">mae</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">mean_absolute_error</span>(<span class="va">y_actual</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"MAE: ${mae:,.0f}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># Easy to interpret!</span>

<span class="cm"># MSE: squared error (less intuitive)</span>
<span class="va">mse</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">mean_squared_error</span>(<span class="va">y_actual</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"MSE: {mse:,.0f}"</span>)

<span class="cm"># RMSE: square root of MSE (back to original units)</span>
<span class="va">rmse</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">sqrt</span>(<span class="va">mse</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"RMSE: ${rmse:,.0f}"</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- RMSE and Scale -->
    <section class="section" id="rmse">
      <span class="section-label">Core Metrics</span>
      <h2>Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Metric Scale</h2>
      <p>RMSE is the square root of MSE, returning error to the original units. This makes it more interpretable than MSE while retaining the mathematical benefits of squaring. RMSE is widely used in machine learning competitions and literature.</p>

      <p>The important insight is that RMSE penalizes large errors more heavily than small errors. An error of 100 contributes 10,000 to MSE, while two errors of 50 each contribute only 5,000. This makes RMSE particularly sensitive to outliers.</p>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <strong>Scale-dependent metrics:</strong> MAE, MSE, and RMSE are all in the same units as your target variable. A RMSE of 10,000 is acceptable for house prices but terrible for predicting item quantities. Always contextualize these metrics relative to the scale of your target variable.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- R-Squared -->
    <section class="section" id="r-squared">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Metrics</span>
      <h2>R-Squared (Coefficient of Determination)</h2>
      <p>R² measures how much variance in the target variable your model explains. It ranges from 0 to 1 (higher is better), and is scale-independent, making it easier to interpret across different problems.</p>

      <p>R² = 1 - (SS_res / SS_tot)</p>
      <p>Where SS_res = Σ(y_actual - y_pred)² and SS_tot = Σ(y_actual - mean(y_actual))²</p>

      <p>Interpretation: An R² of 0.85 means your model explains 85% of the variance in the target variable. An R² of 0.50 means it explains 50%. A naive baseline model that always predicts the mean target value has R² = 0. Models worse than this baseline have negative R².</p>

      <h3>Computing R-Squared</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">r2_score</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">linear_model</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">LinearRegression</span>

<span class="cm"># Train model</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">LinearRegression</span>()
<span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)

<span class="cm"># Predictions</span>
<span class="va">y_pred</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">predict</span>(<span class="va">X_test</span>)

<span class="cm"># R-squared</span>
<span class="va">r2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">r2_score</span>(<span class="va">y_test</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"R² Score: {r2:.3f}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Model explains {r2*100:.1f}% of variance"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Alternative: use model.score() directly</span>
<span class="va">r2_alt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">score</span>(<span class="va">X_test</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"R² Score (via .score()): {r2_alt:.3f}"</span>)</code></pre>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <strong>When to use R²:</strong> Use R² when you need a scale-independent metric and want to explain the percentage of variance explained. Use MAE or RMSE when you need absolute error magnitude in business units (dollars, units sold, etc.).
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- MAPE for Interpretability -->
    <section class="section" id="mape">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Metrics</span>
      <h2>Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE)</h2>
      <p>MAPE expresses error as a percentage of actual values, making it highly interpretable. A MAPE of 5% means predictions are off by 5% on average. This is particularly useful when comparing models across datasets with different scales.</p>

      <p>Formula: MAPE = (1/n) × Σ|y_actual - y_predicted| / |y_actual| × 100</p>

      <h3>Using MAPE</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">mean_absolute_percentage_error</span>

<span class="cm"># Example: Sales forecasting</span>
<span class="va">y_actual</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">1000</span>, <span class="nm">1500</span>, <span class="nm">2000</span>, <span class="nm">800</span>])
<span class="va">y_pred</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">1050</span>, <span class="nm">1450</span>, <span class="nm">2100</span>, <span class="nm">750</span>])

<span class="va">mape</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">mean_absolute_percentage_error</span>(<span class="va">y_actual</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"MAPE: {mape*100:.1f}%"</span>)  <span class="cm"># Very interpretable!</span></code></pre>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <strong>MAPE pitfall:</strong> MAPE can be misleading when actual values are close to zero (division by near-zero). Use with caution for zero-containing datasets.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Choosing Regression Metrics -->
    <section class="section" id="metric-selection">
      <span class="section-label">Practical Application</span>
      <h2>Choosing the Right Regression Metric</h2>

      <h3>House Price Prediction</h3>
      <p><strong>Context:</strong> Predicting house prices ($200k-$1M range)</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended metrics:</strong> RMSE or MAE in dollars (easy to explain to stakeholders), R² for model comparison. An RMSE of $50,000 is interpretable; "model is off by $50k on average."</p>

      <h3>Stock Price Prediction</h3>
      <p><strong>Context:</strong> Predicting daily stock movements (highly volatile)</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended metrics:</strong> MAPE for percentage error (5% error is more meaningful than $5 error), R² for model quality, MAE for actual dollar predictions.</p>

      <h3>Traffic Flow Prediction</h3>
      <p><strong>Context:</strong> Predicting vehicle count (0-1000 vehicles)</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended metrics:</strong> MAE (number of vehicles off) or RMSE if penalizing large errors is important. Scale matters here—10 vehicles off when predicting 100 is terrible; 10 off when predicting 1000 is good.</p>

      <h3>Energy Demand Forecasting</h3>
      <p><strong>Context:</strong> Predicting kilowatt-hours (continuous values)</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended metrics:</strong> MAPE for percentage accuracy (5% error is operationally meaningful), RMSE for magnitude, MAE for average error magnitude.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Complete Evaluation Workflow -->
    <section class="section" id="workflow">
      <span class="section-label">Real-World Application</span>
      <h2>Complete Regression Evaluation Workflow</h2>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">mean_absolute_error</span>, <span class="fn">mean_squared_error</span>, <span class="fn">r2_score</span>, <span class="fn">mean_absolute_percentage_error</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">evaluate_regression</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Comprehensive regression evaluation"""</span>
    <span class="va">mae</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">mean_absolute_error</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
    <span class="va">mse</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">mean_squared_error</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
    <span class="va">rmse</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">sqrt</span>(<span class="va">mse</span>)
    <span class="va">mape</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">mean_absolute_percentage_error</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
    <span class="va">r2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">r2_score</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)

    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">"=== REGRESSION METRICS ==="</span>)
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"MAE:  {mae:,.2f}"</span>)
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"RMSE: {rmse:,.2f}"</span>)
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"MAPE: {mape*100:.2f}%"</span>)
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"R²:   {r2:.3f} (explains {r2*100:.1f}% variance)"</span>)

    <span class="kw">return</span> {<span class="st">'mae'</span>: <span class="va">mae</span>, <span class="st">'rmse'</span>: <span class="va">rmse</span>, <span class="st">'mape'</span>: <span class="va">mape</span>, <span class="st">'r2'</span>: <span class="va">r2</span>}

<span class="cm"># Usage</span>
<span class="fn">evaluate_regression</span>(<span class="va">y_test</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

